From b14594c095833aabc53534a886c96a7bfb9c07d9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Boyd Milan <76000029+jersonboydmilan@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 11:47:59 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] [python] Reject a qvector as the targets of a multi-target
 custom operation

A custom operation with more than one target pulled its targets out of a
qvector argument: custom_swap(q), custom_swap(*q) and custom_swap(q, r) were
all accepted. Nothing else in the Python kernel language does this. Built-in
multi-target operations and user kernels reject these calls, and so do the
custom operation's own .adj and .ctrl paths. In review of #5649 the CUDA-Q
DSL rules were stated: every quantum operation takes its targets as
individual positional arguments, and only a single-target operation has a
qvector (broadcast) overload. #2484, which asked for this convenience, should
not be implemented.

For numTargets > 1, __expandCustomOpTargets now takes each target as its own
positional argument and rejects a qvector or a starred argument at compile
time, naming the operation in the error. The single-target path is unchanged
here; #5649 replaces it with broadcast.

The tests that pinned the old behavior now expect the error. The runtime-sized
case is caught at compile time too, so it no longer needs the macOS arm64 JIT
skip. The note in default_ops.rst is updated to match.

Fixes #5647.

Signed-off-by: Boyd Milan <76000029+jersonboydmilan@users.noreply.github.com>
